{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And Moses called Joshua and said to him before all Israel, etc.</b> But were all the people seeing the voices? What is the meaning of 'and he said before all Israel'? And what is the meaning of 'be strong and courageous' — does it depend on him? For if YHWH does not strengthen his hand, what is Joshua that one should say to him, 'be strong and courageous'? Also, every thoughtful person should reflect on the variations that obtain between the words of Moses to Joshua and the words of the Holy One, blessed be He, to Joshua. For Moses said: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבוֹא אֶת הָעָם״, whereas the Holy One, blessed be He, said: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא״. And that which Rashi explained — that Moses said: the elders of the generation are with you; everything is according to their mind and counsel, therefore he said ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבוֹא״, but the Holy One, blessed be He, said ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא״ — against their will, take a staff and strike them on the head — one may press a difficulty on this: how did the Holy One, blessed be He, contradict the words of Moses, seeing that all his words were spoken in prophecy and from the mouth of YHWH? Moses said: ״אֶת הָעָם״, and the Holy One, blessed be He, said: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״. Moses said: ״אֶל הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּע ה׳ לַאֲבוֹתָם״ — he suspended the inheritance of the land on their fathers, whereas the Holy One, blessed be He, said: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶל הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּעְתִּי לָהֶם״ — and did not suspend it on their fathers. Moses said: ״וְאַתָּה תַּנְחִילֶנָּה אוֹתָם״, and the Holy One, blessed be He, did not say so; rather He said: ״וְאָנֹכִי אֶהְיֶה עִמָּךְ״. All these variations — is it not a matter?" "And I say that this matter appears in my eyes to be exactly like what the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses (Numbers 20:12): ״יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם בִּי לְהַקְדִּישֵׁנִי לְעֵינֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לָכֵן לֹא תָבִיאוּ אֶת הַקָּהָל״ etc. — He began with 'before the children of Israel' and concluded with 'the congregation.' And we have already explained above, in the portion of Ḥukkat (Numbers 20:12), that there were always two factions among Israel: the children of Israel — these are the seed of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, believers and children of believers; but the mixed multitude, and likewise the lowlier elements among Israel, were called by the name 'congregation' (קָהָל) or 'people' (עָם). Now, what Moses sinned in the striking of the rock was that he did not produce, for those who saw it, an effect to bring them to faith in YHWH, to derive an a fortiori argument from the rock, as Rashi explained there, and as it is written: ״יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם בִּי״ — 'because you did not bring to believe in Me.' For had only believing children been there, there would have been no need to perform before their eyes a deed that produces strengthening in faith; but since there was also a congregation of converts there who required strengthening in faith, therefore YHWH insisted and said: ״יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם בִּי לְעֵינֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לָכֵן לֹא תָבִיאוּ אֶת הַקָּהָל״. For had they all been children of Israel, I would not have been concerned; but since among them there is also a congregation of children who are not confirmed in faith, and they always need deeds that will strengthen knees tottering in faith, therefore 'you shall not bring the congregation into the land' — for that congregation needs a man in whom is spirit, who will always perform before their eyes miracles that strengthen hands feeble in faith." "And exactly like this, Moses said to Joshua: ״לְעֵינֵי כָל יִשְׂרָאֵל: חֲזַק וֶאֱמָץ כִּי אַתָּה תָּבוֹא אֶת הָעָם הַזֶּה״, and the phrase ״לְעֵינֵי כָל יִשְׂרָאֵל״ is inverted downward, to say: before all Israel — ״כָּל״ to include the converts and the rejects among the people. Before all of them, be strong and courageous — that is to say: perform before their eyes things that produce strengthening in faith, because you will come with this people, for among the children of Israel there are also rejects called by the name 'people' (עָם), and they are not strong in faith, for they are always bent on evil. Therefore you must perform before them deeds of strengthening and encouragement in faith, so that what happened to me will not happen to you — for on their account it was said to me: 'therefore you shall not bring the congregation into the land, because you did not sanctify the Name before the children of Israel.' Therefore I am warning you: before all Israel you must be strong and courageous in matters that bring about faith, and then you will come with this people into the land, and what happened to me will not happen to you." "And with this proposal all the mentioned variations are resolved. For in Moses' words to Joshua it says: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבוֹא אֶת הָעָם״, and this speaks of the rejects among the people, for the reason we have mentioned. For on their account he needed to warn him: before all Israel, be strong and courageous; and therefore he said: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבוֹא אֶת הָעָם הַזֶּה״ — to say: the elders of the generation are with you; everything is according to their mind and counsel. For indeed ״תֵּחַת גְּעָרָה בְמֵבִין מֵהַכּוֹת כְּסִיל מֵאָה״ (Proverbs 17:10), and the rejects among the people do not accept authority at all, and their way is not to bear the yoke and submit to the words of their leaders. Therefore Moses said: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבוֹא אֶת הָעָם הַזֶּה״ — everything is according to their mind and counsel. And he said: ״אֶל הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּע ה׳ לַאֲבוֹתָם״ etc., for on their own merit they do not deserve to enter the land, but only inasmuch as YHWH swore to their fathers; for the merit of their fathers and your merit will stand for them. Therefore it says: ״אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּע ה׳ לַאֲבוֹתָם לָתֵת לָהֶם וְאַתָּה תַּנְחִילֶנָּה אוֹתָם״ — for your merit too will stand for them to give them the land as an inheritance; but for themselves they have no merit of their own." "But the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Joshua, without qualification: ״חֲזַק וֶאֱמָץ״ — in Torah and commandment, and did not say 'before the children of Israel,' for He is speaking of the worthy ones among Israel who do not need strengthening in faith, and the worthy ones accept the authority of their leaders. Therefore He said: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — for the worthy ones among them, who are called 'children of Israel,' heed the voice of their leaders even regarding a matter that is against their mind, which is as though coerced in action. Therefore it says: ״תָּבִיא״ — against their will, for the worthy ones accept authority and rebuke, as stated. And He said: ״אֶל הָאָרֶץ אֲשֶׁר נִשְׁבַּעְתִּי לָהֶם״ — He did not mention 'to their fathers,' because the worthy ones among Israel merit the land on their own account and do not need the merit of the fathers. And He said: ״וְאָנֹכִי אֶהְיֶה עִמָּךְ״, for the merit of Israel caused that 'I will be with you' — for all the leaders of Israel do not ascend to greatness except by the merit of Israel, as it is written (Exodus 28:1): ״וְאַתָּה הַקְרֵב אֵלֶיךָ אֶת אַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ מִתּוֹךְ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״." "And some say that Moshe taught him the way of civil governance, which must be according to their [the people's] understanding and counsel, for the reason explained in parashat Pinḥas (Numbers 27:18) in the verse ״וְסָמַכְתָּ אֶת יָדְךָ עָלָיו״. And the proof for this is the end of that verse, which says: ״וְאַתָּה תַּנְחִילֶנָּה אוֹתָם״. But the Holy One, blessed be He, taught him the way of governance in Torah and commandment, as is said at the end of this verse: ״וְאָנֹכִי אֶהְיֶה עִמָּךְ״, and with this it is surely not applicable to say that everything should be according to their understanding, but rather: take a staff, etc. — to compel them to the keeping of the Torah." "Another matter: ״לְעֵינֵי כָל יִשְׂרָאֵל חֲזַק וֶאֱמָץ״ — before the eyes of all Israel, be strong and resolute — for the eyes of all Israel are upon him, and from him they will see and so will they do; therefore he must especially strengthen himself in Torah and fear of HaShem before their eyes, along the lines of קְשֹׁט עַצְמְךָ תְּחִלָּה (Bava Metzi'a 107a), for he is like a peg upon which everything hangs. And this is what it says: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבוֹא אֶת הָעָם״ — that is to say, the worthy among Israel do not suspect the upright as much as do the lesser ones, and for their sake you must be all the more careful before their eyes."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:50.970270616Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}